<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idris of Libya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as King Idris I, Idris al-Senussi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, Muhammad Idris al-Senussi, was head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senussi-order">Senussi order</a> and leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>. Exiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1922 during the Italian conquest, he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, became emir of Cyrenaica in 1949 and king of the independent United Kingdom of Libya in 1951. Oil was found during his reign, but he was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a>'s coup in 1969 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idris-of-libya.jpg" alt="Idris of Libya" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Idris_I_of_Libya.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Idris was born at Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaghbub">Jaghbub</a>, the headquarters of the Senussi movement, the son of Sayyid Muhammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> al-Senussi and his third wife. His grandfather, Sayyid Muhammad ibn Ali as-Senussi, founded the Senussi Sufi order, a revivalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> movement based largely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>. By the end of the 19th century the order had set up a government in Cyrenaica that united its tribes, controlled its pilgrimage and trade routes and collected taxes. In 1916 Idris became head of the order when his cousin Ahmed Sharif as-Senussi abdicated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ahmed Sharif had attacked British forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Ottoman pressure. Idris stopped the attacks and made a tacit alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> that lasted half a century. Through British intermediaries he negotiated with the Italians, who had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> in 1913, and agreements at al-Zuwaytina and Akrama left most of inland Cyrenaica under the Senussi. In 1920 the Accord of al-Rajma made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Cyrenaica with autonomy over several oases. In 1922 Tripolitanian leaders asked him to extend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> into Tripolitania, and he agreed in November. Fearing Italian retaliation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>, he went into exile in Egypt in December 1922.</p><p>The Italian reconquest followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>'s livestock was decimated, much of its population was interned in camps, and an estimated 12,000 Cyrenaicans were executed in 1930 and 1931. In the Second World War Idris supported the United Kingdom, and a Libyan Arab Force of volunteers served in support and gendarmerie roles. After the war British and French forces governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> resolved in November 1949 that Libya should become a single independent state under Idris as king by January 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Idris proclaimed the United Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> from Benghazi on 24 December 1951. The country had about one million people and was one of the poorest in the world, with an illiteracy rate of 94 percent. It was federal, with Benghazi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> as joint capitals. Idris banned political parties, and from 1952 all candidates were government nominees. In 1963 he abolished the federal system and the country became the Kingdom of Libya. He allowed British and American air bases in return for aid. Large oil reserves found in 1959 raised per capita income from $25 to $35 in 1951 to $2,000 by 1969, and Libya joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a> in 1962.</p><p>Idris was deposed on 1 September 1969, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> for medical treatment, by army officers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a>; the coup came a day before his intended abdication. He went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and was sentenced to death in absentia in November 1971. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1983 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. He had married five times, and none of his children survived childhood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Idris of Libya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under a UN resolution, Libya became independent on 24 December 1951 as a federal kingdom uniting Tripolitania, Cyrenaica and the Fezzan, with <b>Idris al-Senussi</b>, ruler of the Emirate of Cyrenaica, as King Idris I.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-libya">Kingdom of Libya</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied with Britain in the Second World War, <b>Idris al-Senussi</b> became ruler of the Emirate of Cyrenaica in 1949 and king of the Kingdom of Libya in 1951.</blockquote><a href="/state/senussi-order">Senussi Order</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaghbub">Jaghbub</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.74,24.52&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Jaghbub on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5188 1077 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Idris of Libya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5638 1347h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5638 1347h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.74,24.52&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
